WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.547
Let’s use the actual workbench. The three columns are three perspectives on the same

00:00:05.547 --> 00:00:11.095
application: its platform, an organization, and a customer. Shipments are our sample

00:00:11.095 --> 00:00:16.444
data. The product we are demonstrating is the rules and lenses engine underneath.

00:00:16.444 --> 00:00:22.058
Start with Lenses. At each stage, we need to distinguish the surfaces we receive from

00:00:22.058 --> 00:00:24.700
the surfaces we author for someone else.

00:00:29.704 --> 00:00:35.350
The platform starts from its schema. It creates a lens called Shipment visibility.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:41.203
Open it and look at the visible fields: internal cost is excluded. The definition tab

00:00:41.203 --> 00:00:46.505
shows the same choice as JSON. This is behavior expressed as data. The engine

00:00:46.505 --> 00:00:52.426
interprets it. A platform can also compose a new lens from an existing one, preserving

00:00:52.426 --> 00:00:53.804
the parent’s limits.

00:00:58.806 --> 00:01:04.472
The organization can see the lens it received from the platform, with its organization

00:01:04.472 --> 00:01:09.611
identity bound into it. Below that is My shipments, the lens this organization

00:01:09.611 --> 00:01:15.145
authored for customers. The customer sees available lenses grouped by provider. This

00:01:15.145 --> 00:01:20.548
distinction matters: receiving a surface gives you a starting point. Delegating it

00:01:20.548 --> 00:01:24.830
adds restrictions; it cannot undo the restrictions you inherited.

00:01:29.831 --> 00:01:35.551
Now open the platform’s data. There are three organizations. Open Andes and follow its

00:01:35.551 --> 00:01:41.139
shipments, then open one shipment and inspect its events. These are related records,

00:01:41.139 --> 00:01:46.594
not flattened labels. The organization’s own data view contains its shipments. The

00:01:46.594 --> 00:01:51.915
customer’s view contains only the records returned through its permitted lenses.

00:01:51.915 --> 00:01:56.306
Including a relation lets you inspect the history behind a result.

00:02:01.308 --> 00:02:06.538
In the organization’s data view, choose One rule, two paths. Begin with a single

00:02:06.538 --> 00:02:12.031
condition: status equals delayed. Run both. On the left, Check evaluates the allowed

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:17.131
records in memory. On the right, To Prisma translates that same condition into

00:02:17.131 --> 00:02:22.361
database filters and fetches matching records. Compare the results: the same six

00:02:22.361 --> 00:02:27.919
shipments, with the same permitted fields and events. There is one rule definition to

00:02:27.919 --> 00:02:28.508
maintain.

00:02:33.509 --> 00:02:39.046
Replace the simple condition with Relation counts. Now the rule asks for at least two

00:02:39.046 --> 00:02:44.453
events where both tests pass: customs hold, and at least twelve hours of delay. The

00:02:44.453 --> 00:02:50.055
conditions are nested inside the relation. Run both again. Inspect the generated plan:

00:02:50.055 --> 00:02:55.658
this question needs an event grouping step, then a shipment query. The engine performs

00:02:55.658 --> 00:03:00.608
that translation. You changed the JSON, without writing a new query handler.

00:03:05.612 --> 00:03:11.330
Before asking an AI to write a predicate, let it discover its surface. Here we inspect

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:17.048
the customer’s available fields and their meanings. This metadata response is supplied

00:03:17.048 --> 00:03:22.434
by the application. Now try using internal cost, a field the parent excluded. The

00:03:22.434 --> 00:03:28.020
request is rejected. The engine checks the proposed rule against the lens; knowing a

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.212
field’s name does not make that field available.

00:03:36.212 --> 00:03:41.517
Next, run a customer rule that uses event delay hours. It is valid and returns

00:03:41.517 --> 00:03:47.300
matches. Now the platform narrows the upstream lens by removing delay hours. Open the

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:53.150
old query again. The validator marks the rule stale and identifies the field it can no

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:58.796
longer use. This is not information aging. The surface that made the rule valid has

00:03:58.796 --> 00:04:03.762
changed. Revise the rule to use an available field, and it can run again.

00:04:08.763 --> 00:04:14.623
Open Access. Each lens has a policy built from conditions, and the inspector shows who

00:04:14.623 --> 00:04:20.345
passes it. The platform requires an active Pro organization. Change Andes to Starter

00:04:20.345 --> 00:04:25.796
and watch its inherited lens disappear, along with the customer’s lens from that

00:04:25.796 --> 00:04:31.587
provider. The source records still exist. Access changed because the engine evaluated

00:04:31.587 --> 00:04:37.378
different inputs. Rules, lenses, and permissions are dynamic JSON building blocks for

00:04:37.378 --> 00:04:38.264
your AI SaaS.
